## 5. Deviations / notes for H1

- **`docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md`**: beyond the two directive-cited spots, I also generalized the file-backend frame's "digest is a raw 32-byte SHA-256 byte string" wording (it's the address-carried algorithm's digest now) and the "SHA-256 collision resistance" acceptance-criteria line, since both are address-algorithm claims the architect's directive ("no longer claim SHA-256 is the sole DaoJing address algorithm") covers even though they weren't in the cited line ranges.
- The contract-test file is necessarily self-contained: since `dao.jing` itself gains no registry/`segment-matches?`/BLAKE3 support until H1, the test defines a private reference implementation of the frozen grammar/parser/predicate rules and pins it against fixtures — plus the *real* `dao.jing/sha256-bytes`, `canonical-bytes`, `content-hash`, and `segment-key`, which don't change shape in H0. H1 should replace the reference `parse-segment-address`/`segment-matches?` with calls into the real `dao.jing` functions; the fixtures and pinned behavior shouldn't need to change.
- The architectural-lint test specifies the lint's decision procedure (syntactic, fully-qualified-var-based, not data-flow) against representative forms; it is not a repository-wide sweep, since today's real Class-3 sites are still non-conforming pending H1.
- `test/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn`'s canonical-value rows are pinned to the current order-normalized print encoder at HEAD `ba88f769`; the contract test's `fixtures-load-and-agree-with-canonical-bytes` guards this and will need re-generation alongside every other DaoJing fixture when the CBOR clean break lands, per `dao.jing.cbor.md`'s existing ruling.
